PRIVATE REFLECTION: {
- Core question: Identify which peptide among the options binds or is highly likely to bind an integrin receptor in an in vitro assay.
- Consensus status: Complete unamity across all colleagues for Choice B (RGDSPSS). Previously dissenting colleagues who initially considered C (RGDLTTP) or A (RGDMAA) have fully converged onto B.
- Biochemical rationale: Choice B incorporates the canonical fibronectin 10th type III repeat sequence (RGDSP), a gold-standard active motif that forms the required beta-turn for robust integrin pocket docking (e.g., alpha_5 beta_1, alpha_v beta_3) without requiring secondary structural scaffolding. Alternative motifs like RGDL typically necessitate an extended amphipathic alpha-helical consensus (RGDLxxL/I) absent in linear heptapeptide RGDLTTP.
- Conclusion: Firmly resolved on B.
}

